1
00:00:00,060 --> 00:00:02,690
Imagine you're building a video game. You have

2
00:00:02,690 --> 00:00:04,900
two players. Each player has a name, a

3
00:00:04,900 --> 00:00:07,830
health level, and actions like taking damage or

4
00:00:07,830 --> 00:00:11,100
healing. Now imagine managing a hundred players using

5
00:00:11,100 --> 00:00:13,840
separate variables and functions that would get messy

6
00:00:13,840 --> 00:00:16,800
pretty quickly, and this is exactly where Python

7
00:00:16,800 --> 00:00:19,490
classes become useful. A class is simply a

8
00:00:19,490 --> 00:00:22,680
blueprint for creating objects. Think of a blueprint

9
00:00:22,680 --> 00:00:24,930
for a house. The blueprint isn't a house

10
00:00:24,930 --> 00:00:27,260
itself, but you can build many houses from

11
00:00:27,260 --> 00:00:30,030
it. In Python, the idea is similar. Let's

12
00:00:30,030 --> 00:00:32,600
create a class called player. We start with

13
00:00:32,600 --> 00:00:35,830
the keyword class, followed by its name. Inside

14
00:00:35,830 --> 00:00:38,250
this class, we define something called the init

15
00:00:38,250 --> 00:00:41,160
method. Don't worry about the strange double underscore.

16
00:00:41,160 --> 00:00:43,700
This method runs automatically whenever we create a

17
00:00:43,700 --> 00:00:45,300
new player. It helps us set up the

18
00:00:45,300 --> 00:00:48,650
player's initial information. Here, we give every player

19
00:00:48,650 --> 00:00:50,700
a name and a starting health of one

20
00:00:50,700 --> 00:00:54,310
hundred. But what exactly is self? Self refers

21
00:00:54,310 --> 00:00:56,700
to the specific object we're working with. If

22
00:00:56,700 --> 00:00:59,100
we create two players, each one gets its

23
00:00:59,100 --> 00:01:01,640
own name and health. So changing one player's

24
00:01:01,640 --> 00:01:04,160
health won't change the others. Now let's make

25
00:01:04,160 --> 00:01:07,260
our players actually do something. Inside the class,

26
00:01:07,260 --> 00:01:09,390
we create a method called take damage. A

27
00:01:09,390 --> 00:01:12,030
method is simply a function defined inside a

28
00:01:12,030 --> 00:01:14,470
class. This method takes an amount of damage

29
00:01:14,470 --> 00:01:16,650
and subtracts it from the player's health. We

30
00:01:16,650 --> 00:01:19,120
can also create a heal method that increases

31
00:01:19,120 --> 00:01:21,980
health. And finally, a show status method to

32
00:01:21,980 --> 00:01:25,840
display the player's current information. Notice something interesting.

33
00:01:25,900 --> 00:01:27,950
We've grouped the player's data and the actions

34
00:01:27,950 --> 00:01:30,200
that operate on that data in one place.

35
00:01:30,200 --> 00:01:32,590
That's the power of a class. Now comes

36
00:01:32,590 --> 00:01:35,400
the fun part. Let's create our first object.

37
00:01:35,400 --> 00:01:37,660
We write player, pass in the name Alex,

38
00:01:37,660 --> 00:01:40,040
and store the result in a variable. Just

39
00:01:40,040 --> 00:01:42,100
like that, we've created a player with a

40
00:01:42,100 --> 00:01:45,250
name and 100 health points. Let's create another

41
00:01:45,250 --> 00:01:47,790
player named Sam. Both objects come from the

42
00:01:47,790 --> 00:01:51,150
same class, but they're completely independent. Now Alex

43
00:01:51,150 --> 00:01:54,480
takes 30 damage. His health drops to 70.

44
00:01:54,540 --> 00:01:56,800
When we heal him by 15, his health

45
00:01:56,800 --> 00:02:00,580
becomes 85. Meanwhile, Sam takes 10 damage, leaving

46
00:02:00,580 --> 00:02:03,200
him with 90. When we display their status,

47
00:02:03,200 --> 00:02:05,680
we get two different results. And that's the

48
00:02:05,680 --> 00:02:08,230
key idea. The class defines what a player

49
00:02:08,230 --> 00:02:10,110
is and what a player can do. An

50
00:02:10,110 --> 00:02:13,040
object is an actual instance created from that

51
00:02:13,040 --> 00:02:16,610
class. Attributes hold the object's information, methods define

52
00:02:16,610 --> 00:02:19,640
its behavior, and self-connects those methods to the

53
00:02:19,640 --> 00:02:23,100
correct object. Once you understand this, classes stop

54
00:02:23,100 --> 00:02:25,900
looking like complicated syntax. They become a way

55
00:02:25,900 --> 00:02:29,280
to organize code into reusable, understandable pieces, and

56
00:02:29,280 --> 00:02:31,280
that's your introduction to classes in Python.

